On Jan 23, 3:44 pm, David Morgan <use...@deejayem.net> wrote:
>
> Perhaps I was overly brief before. As I see it, nethack's alignments are
> a sort of flattening of the D&D ones onto one axis.

Agree.

> When polymorphed into a demon, you find the idea of praying to a lawful
> god repugnant (although Asmodeus (and maybe one other prince?) is
> lawful). Is it fair to say that demons are evil rather than chaotic?

This is a stealing from D&D and the distinction between demons and
devils. Demons in D&D are CE, Devils are LE.  We don't have devils as
a separate monster.  But we still have Baalzebub, Geryon, and Asmodeus
as lawful beings. I don't think they're good.


>
> Holy water hurts chaotics - holiness has much more to do with good/evil
> than lawful/chaotic.

Here's a puzzler.  I agree  that the intent here is to make chaotics
"usually evil".  So why is it, when  my character prays on a chaotic
altar as a chaotic, I get holy water? Shouldn't a chaotic god give
unholy water to a worshipper?  Instead, praying to a chaotic God as a
chaotic worshippers gives holy water, which might kill my character if
(s)he drinks it.

I agree with you that chaotic is the alignment to play if you want to
play an evil character. You skip out on lots of penalties for murder,
theft, and human sacrifice. You also have an easier time with the
mysterious force.

Still,  if lawful=good and chaotic=evil, there are some glaring
inconsistencies. We have chaotic beings in Heaven and lawful beings
ruling levels of Hell. And I think that's deliberate.  I speculate
that the dev team wanted to make Chaotic an alignment such that, if
you were so inclined, you could play an evil character and not get
punished for it.  OTOH, you aren't forced to play an evil character as
a chaotic if you don't want to. Thus the game is made more appealing,
turning off neither those players that want to play evil characters
nor those characters who want to play good rogues and rangers.

